And we want that to change the way we live our lives today.

108
00:06:11,500 --> 00:06:16,960
We want that to change the way we interact with our neighbors, our coworkers, our friends

109
00:06:16,960 --> 00:06:24,280
and our enemies so that we can see more visibly your kingdom on earth as it is in heaven, the

110
00:06:24,280 --> 00:06:25,120
place where you dwell.

111
00:06:25,120 --> 00:06:29,240
God, teach us more and more about what heaven is like today.

112
00:06:30,080 --> 00:06:36,420
About how you rule and reign and how we can participate in your kingdom on earth as it is in heaven.

113
00:06:36,420 --> 00:06:38,300
In Jesus' name I pray.

114
00:06:38,300 --> 00:06:39,100
Amen.

115
00:06:39,100 --> 00:06:42,380
So I went to Bible college.

116
00:06:42,380 --> 00:06:44,720
Thank you.

117
00:06:44,720 --> 00:06:51,940
And Bible college was interesting, but I remember one class most in particular that was very interesting.

118
00:06:51,940 --> 00:06:54,780
It was my English literature class my freshman year.

119
00:06:54,780 --> 00:06:57,880
And we had a professor by the name of Mr. France.

120
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:00,740
And Mr. France was a grad student.

121
00:07:00,740 --> 00:07:04,860
And so he was like, he was working on probably a PhD or something.

122
00:07:04,860 --> 00:07:09,120
And he was still, he was teaching classes probably to get free schooling, which is great.

123
00:07:09,120 --> 00:07:15,700
He was kind of a nerd, like the type of nerd that translates Egyptian hieroglyphics in his free time.

124
00:07:17,660 --> 00:07:21,440
And so, but he was young and smart and a student.

125
00:07:21,440 --> 00:07:25,720
But because of all of those things, he wasn't the best professor in the world.

126
00:07:25,720 --> 00:07:31,980
Like I remember one class in particular, we, all of us were there on time.

127
00:07:31,980 --> 00:07:33,060
The class started at 10.

128
00:07:33,740 --> 00:07:42,620
And at about 10.10, Mr. France skateboarded into the classroom with his tie like disheveled and two buttons on his shirt undone.

129
00:07:42,620 --> 00:07:47,520
He was putting his contacts in at the front of the class 10 minutes late.

130
00:07:47,520 --> 00:07:50,420
And you could tell that he had not prepared for the class at all.

131
00:07:50,420 --> 00:07:53,500
And so he decided to put, this was his lesson plan for the day.

132
00:07:53,980 --> 00:07:58,040
He played us a nine inch nails song while he was putting his contacts in.

133
00:07:58,040 --> 00:08:07,640
And when he finished his contacts in his tie, he was like, all right, let's do some literary analysis about what this song means.

134
00:08:07,640 --> 00:08:09,900
What is the symbolism in this song?

135
00:08:09,900 --> 00:08:19,200
And I'll never forget this because I learned a couple valuable lessons that day, believe it or not, from the professor that showed up 10 minutes late on a skateboard, which was fun.

136
00:08:19,900 --> 00:08:22,780
I understood, I learned two valuable lessons that day.

137
00:08:22,780 --> 00:08:33,220
And the first valuable lesson is that literary analysis is difficult, especially when there's poetic imagery and artistic symbolism.

138
00:08:33,220 --> 00:08:41,900
Like it's hard to really grasp what it is that the author is trying to communicate when there's symbols and artistic expressions in them.

139
00:08:41,900 --> 00:08:45,860
So that's the first thing I learned is that it's difficult.

140
00:08:45,860 --> 00:08:48,320
And the second thing that I learned is that I'm not very good at it.

141
00:08:49,540 --> 00:09:01,920
So, the reason I bring that up is because when we come to the book of Revelation, and this is, we're going to spend some time in Revelation today because it's probably the most condensed exploration of heaven and the end times in all of the Bible.

142
00:09:01,920 --> 00:09:08,860
And I think the problem is that we often misunderstand the book of Revelation because, has anybody read it recently?

143
00:09:08,860 --> 00:09:13,760
Pretty confusing, difficult, maybe hard to understand.

144
00:09:13,760 --> 00:09:16,320
And some of you are like, no, no, no, it's not hard to understand at all.

145
00:09:16,320 --> 00:09:17,580
It's really easy to understand.

146
00:09:20,080 --> 00:09:21,920
Send me an email, let's have a meeting, okay?

147
00:09:21,920 --> 00:09:26,200
So, there's a problem in the American church.

148
00:09:26,200 --> 00:09:32,480
One is we either ignore it because it's confusing and difficult and challenging and we just get too scared and overwhelmed and so we don't read it.

149
00:09:32,480 --> 00:09:34,820
Or some of us, we become obsessed with it.

150
00:09:34,820 --> 00:09:37,420
It becomes the primary thing that we read and consume.

151
00:09:37,420 --> 00:09:43,880
And because of some of the material that is out there on the book of Revelation right now in our modern cultural moment,

152
00:09:43,960 --> 00:09:48,100
a lot of us end up being obsessed with it but misunderstanding what it actually is all about.

153
00:09:48,100 --> 00:09:53,680
And so, I think many Christians in the American church, we either totally ignore it or we misunderstand it.

154
00:09:53,680 --> 00:09:58,440
And my goal for today is twofold, and this is why I said it was ambitious.

155
00:09:58,640 --> 00:10:05,680
I want to give us some big picture principles for how we can better understand the book of Revelation as a whole.

156
00:10:06,440 --> 00:10:14,160
And then I want to hop into two specific chapters and kind of take those principles and show you how it works from two chapters specifically.

157
00:10:14,160 --> 00:10:16,460
Does that sound like an okay plan?

158
00:10:16,460 --> 00:10:17,660
Okay.

159
00:10:17,840 --> 00:10:21,120
We did something new today on the back of your bulletins.

160
00:10:21,120 --> 00:10:22,740
There's fill-in-the-blank notes.

161
00:10:22,740 --> 00:10:26,680
So, for all my type A people, there you go.

162
00:10:26,680 --> 00:10:28,240
I can't promise this will happen every week.

163
00:10:28,240 --> 00:10:33,300
We make our bulletins on Thursday morning, and that would mean that I would have to finish my sermon by Thursday morning every week.

164
00:10:33,300 --> 00:10:35,680
It's an ambitious task.

165
00:10:35,680 --> 00:10:36,580
I will attempt it.

166
00:10:36,580 --> 00:10:41,160
But for now, you have one bulletin with notes on it.

167
00:10:41,160 --> 00:10:46,520
We're going to look first at a couple principles of how to better understand the book of Revelation because it's filled with symbolism.

168
00:10:46,960 --> 00:10:51,600
It's from a totally different culture and a genre of literature that we're not familiar with.

169
00:10:51,600 --> 00:10:59,460
And so, we need some guiding principles when we come to the book of Revelation, and then we'll unpack a couple chapters once we get there.

170
00:10:59,460 --> 00:11:05,560
First, in your notes, if you've taken them, the first principle that I would give you as you interact with the book of Revelation.

171
00:11:05,560 --> 00:11:10,220
Also, I forgot to say, after that, at the very bottom, there's some resources.

172
00:11:10,220 --> 00:11:16,260
So, if this piques your interest at all, and you're like, I want to study this book that I've been afraid to study for a long time,

173
00:11:16,580 --> 00:11:18,140
there's a couple of resources there.

174
00:11:18,140 --> 00:11:19,180
Feel free to pick.

175
00:11:19,180 --> 00:11:19,900
I would pick one.

176
00:11:19,900 --> 00:11:26,480
Don't get like, don't go to the Amazon cart and put all four of them in there today because then you'll just be overwhelmed and you won't start it.

177
00:11:26,480 --> 00:11:27,060
But, okay.

178
00:11:27,060 --> 00:11:30,040
Number one, first principle for the book of Revelation.

179
00:11:30,040 --> 00:11:38,820
You and I are not the main characters of the book of Revelation, and the 21st century is not the primary setting.

180
00:11:38,820 --> 00:11:41,460
Does that upset anyone?

181
00:11:41,460 --> 00:11:42,420
Don't tell me out loud.

182
00:11:42,420 --> 00:11:43,360
We'll talk about it later.

183
00:11:43,360 --> 00:11:50,780
See, the book of Revelation was written for us, but it was written for us, but it was not written to us.

184
00:11:50,820 --> 00:11:58,020
We have to understand when we get to books of the Bible, we talked about this in the first message, we have to understand it in light of the original audience.

185
00:11:58,020 --> 00:12:03,080
What was the original author intending to communicate to the original recipient?

186
00:12:04,120 --> 00:12:13,180
That should drive our understanding of the book of Revelation, and this might seem obvious, but the American church, we struggle to do this, I think, with the book of Revelation more than any other book in the Bible.

187
00:12:13,180 --> 00:12:21,840
We struggle with it with lots of books of the Bible because my friend, she's not my friend, she's famous, but I like to call famous people that I listen to on podcasts my friends.

188
00:12:21,840 --> 00:12:22,720
Anybody else do that?

189
00:12:22,720 --> 00:12:24,080
Okay.

190
00:12:24,080 --> 00:12:31,100
Because my friend Jen Wilkin likes to say that we have main character energy, the American church, right?

191
00:12:31,100 --> 00:12:32,980
We have main character energy.

192
00:12:32,980 --> 00:12:36,760
We love to put ourselves at the center of every story.

193
00:12:36,760 --> 00:12:39,780
It is just by the nature of who we are as Americans.

194
00:12:39,780 --> 00:12:47,420
We love to be at the center of the story, and we love to put ourselves at the center of the book of Revelation.

195
00:12:47,420 --> 00:12:49,740
I think there's a lot of reasons for this.

196
00:12:49,740 --> 00:12:51,360
A couple of reasons.

197
00:12:51,360 --> 00:12:59,880
Our imaginations have been captured and influenced by a popular fiction story that was a New York Times bestseller called Left Behind.

198
00:12:59,880 --> 00:13:00,860
Anybody read those?

199
00:13:00,860 --> 00:13:01,920
Yeah, right?

200
00:13:01,920 --> 00:13:02,600
Okay.

201
00:13:02,600 --> 00:13:06,140
So that book is a, look it up, science fiction book.

202
00:13:06,140 --> 00:13:07,940
Okay.

203
00:13:08,360 --> 00:13:11,780
This book, not a science fiction book.

204
00:13:11,780 --> 00:13:12,960
Okay.

205
00:13:12,960 --> 00:13:13,760
All right.

206
00:13:13,760 --> 00:13:15,060
Just, so we're all in the same.

207
00:13:15,060 --> 00:13:17,600
And if you like, if you like the Left Behind series, that's great.

208
00:13:17,600 --> 00:13:18,020
It's fine.

209
00:13:18,020 --> 00:13:29,800
It is, it is imaginative and cool and there's symbols, but, but we should not use the Left Behind series to help us interpret the book of Revelation because it is a science fiction novel.

210
00:13:29,800 --> 00:13:34,780
I actually read on Amazon today, the New York Times, this is what they said about the Left Behind series.

211
00:13:34,940 --> 00:13:44,920
It combines Tom Clancy-like suspense with touches of romance, high-tech flash, and biblical references.

212
00:13:44,920 --> 00:13:49,200
That's the description of the book.

213
00:13:49,200 --> 00:13:53,100
And some of you did not grow up in the church and you're like, what is this guy talking about?

214
00:13:53,180 --> 00:13:53,380
Okay.

215
00:13:53,380 --> 00:13:55,840
Don't worry about it.

216
00:13:55,840 --> 00:14:00,840
You don't, you don't have the baggage that we have and that is okay.

217
00:14:00,840 --> 00:14:05,260
But anyway, we make ourselves the main character.

218
00:14:05,260 --> 00:14:09,300
Number one, when we're reading Revelation, you and I are not the main characters of the story.

219
00:14:09,300 --> 00:14:12,260
The 21st century is not the primary setting.

220
00:14:13,140 --> 00:14:22,180
Also, I see a lot of modern-day prophets online that are doing their best to interpret the book of Revelation in light of our current cultural moment.

221
00:14:22,180 --> 00:14:27,780
Again, that is making us the primary characters in the primary setting of the book.

222
00:14:27,780 --> 00:14:29,200
And the thing, here's the thing about numbers.

223
00:14:29,200 --> 00:14:38,920
Any of you that work with numbers, any of you that read statistics or studies online, have you ever read a right-leaning study?

224
00:14:39,600 --> 00:14:43,700
And then went and looked for a left-leaning study about the same thing.

225
00:14:43,700 --> 00:14:50,040
And they have the exact same numbers, but they use those numbers to communicate something totally different.

